VALUES ('161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 'cpt-practice-4', 'CPT Coding Proficiency Practice Paper 4', 'cpt', ARRAY['Observation E/M', 'Trauma Anesthesia', 'Neurosurgery', 'Hernia Repair', 'MRI/PET Contrast', 'Endocrine Panels', 'Physical Therapy', 'Global Period Modifiers']::TEXT[], 100, 'advanced', true, 4, 150);

-- ── Section: Evaluation & Management (20 questions) — Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines covering observation status and split/shared visit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank. ──

VALUES ('013c7ae5-98cb-4c94-b55c-2755cc85fb28', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 1, 'Which of the following best describes ''observation care'' E/M coding, as distinguished from inpatient hospital care coding?');
VALUES ('013c7ae5-98cb-4c94-b55c-2755cc85fb28', 'c5477709-566d-4af6-b2cb-a3d49a8bd20a', 'Observation status reflects a distinct clinical/administrative determination from formal inpatient admission (with real coding and payment implications, e.g., under the Two-Midnight Rule concepts already discussed) even when the physical location doesn''t change — CPT''s E/M coding structure reflects this status distinction.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines covering observation status and split/shared visit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('5af6cc40-2c0b-463a-8d0d-edeb3bc93f19', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 2, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding when a patient''s status changes from observation to inpatient during the same hospital stay?');
VALUES ('5af6cc40-2c0b-463a-8d0d-edeb3bc93f19', 'dfeca198-798e-4632-9c15-5de5349f5c3e', 'This reinforces the same status-change coding principle already established (from the CCS-adjacent Two-Midnight Rule/status-conversion content) applied specifically to CPT E/M code selection — the actual documented timeline of status should govern which specific E/M codes apply to which days of the stay.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines covering observation status and split/shared visit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('5379ac9e-1f91-4534-8e50-0043bf33bccf', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 3, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for a physician''s ''observation or inpatient care, including admission and discharge, same date'' service, as distinguished from separately coding an admission and a later, different-day discharge?');
VALUES ('5379ac9e-1f91-4534-8e50-0043bf33bccf', '4e68f55a-e3f0-4414-9ee3-4c14f3e778a7', 'This reapplies the Paper 3 same-day admission/discharge coding concept (already established for standard inpatient hospital care) to the observation/inpatient combined code context — a specific combined code exists for this same-day scenario rather than requiring artificial separation into two full-service codes.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines covering observation status and split/shared visit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('f06c7603-7fd4-426b-8bcd-73e43cb30625', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 4, 'Which of the following best describes the ''substantive portion'' test used to determine which provider''s identity is used for billing a split/shared visit, under current CMS policy?');
VALUES ('f06c7603-7fd4-426b-8bcd-73e43cb30625', 'b6ed0bd2-5920-4c0d-a218-108bfe77b8f8', 'This reinforces the Paper 2 note that CMS''s substantive-portion definition for split/shared visits has evolved over time (time-based or MDM-element-based, depending on the applicable year''s policy) — coders should verify current guidance rather than assuming a permanently fixed definition or an arbitrary, contribution-unrelated tiebreaker.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines covering observation status and split/shared visit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('3641f773-384a-41fe-a7e1-79ff9b53b0cc', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 5, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable documentation practice to support correct split/shared visit billing under the time-based substantive-portion standard?');
VALUES ('3641f773-384a-41fe-a7e1-79ff9b53b0cc', 'b7a02b70-ba94-49e2-8ca9-14bb31cb9e5a', 'This reinforces the general time-documentation principle applied specifically to the split/shared substantive-portion test — accurate application of a time-based standard requires each practitioner''s actual contributed time to be individually documented and comparable.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines covering observation status and split/shared visit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('3e8006d3-c106-4aa3-a1cd-e78f36e14f79', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 6, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding when a hospitalist physician and a nurse practitioner in the same group both see a patient on the same inpatient day, with the physician performing a brief check-in and the NP performing the substantive majority of the visit''s MDM-related work?');
VALUES ('3e8006d3-c106-4aa3-a1cd-e78f36e14f79', '50940f63-2650-4f60-9d73-d2ac1ab9d365', 'This applies the substantive-portion principle concretely — when the NP genuinely performed the substantive majority of the qualifying work, billing reflects that reality (NP''s NPI), rather than an automatic physician-default assumption whenever any physician involvement exists at all.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines covering observation status and split/shared visit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('5eaf876e-912c-4529-9041-912ed86eb2a1', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 7, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for ''prolonged clinical staff service with physician supervision'' when applied during an office visit, as distinguished from prolonged PHYSICIAN E/M time (Paper 1) and prolonged CRITICAL CARE time (Paper 3)?');
VALUES ('5eaf876e-912c-4529-9041-912ed86eb2a1', '6492ef1b-c488-4465-a4f7-bead70a204d1', 'This connects and distinguishes the three distinct prolonged-service code families discussed across this content (physician E/M time, critical care time, and clinical staff time) — reinforcing that ''prolonged services'' is a category concept implemented via several distinct, context-specific code families, not one universal add-on code.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines covering observation status and split/shared visit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('d179ae24-4643-44c6-87e2-e3145e8399ea', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 8, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for an emergency department E/M visit where the patient leaves against medical advice (AMA) before the medical screening examination is fully completed?');
VALUES ('d179ae24-4643-44c6-87e2-e3145e8399ea', 'd79d9d94-a9d6-4bd5-9105-48dd92e558b4', 'This reinforces the general ''code reflects actual documented work'' principle applied to an AMA-departure scenario — the appropriate E/M level is based on what was actually performed/documented before the patient left, not an automatic default to either extreme.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines covering observation status and split/shared visit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('8d8e13fc-32fe-4fbe-b24f-86fe512bd01c', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 9, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable documentation practice specific to an AMA departure from the ED, connecting to the EMTALA discussion from the CCS-adjacent content?');
VALUES ('8d8e13fc-32fe-4fbe-b24f-86fe512bd01c', '4c80cc27-bf26-4ea0-b6b7-054f79890e2e', 'This connects the E/M documentation-sufficiency theme to the EMTALA-adjacent compliance content — thorough AMA documentation (risks discussed, extent of workup completed) serves both the compliance/risk-management purpose and the E/M-coding-support purpose of accurately reflecting the actually-completed work.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines covering observation status and split/shared visit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('a3273ecb-2f3c-4af3-8a1f-3cbbb4ed4a93', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 10, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for a pediatric critical care transport service (a physician/team accompanying a critically ill child during interfacility transport)?');
VALUES ('a3273ecb-2f3c-4af3-8a1f-3cbbb4ed4a93', '8d144617-2498-47a5-84f4-1480db448014', 'Critical care transport (physician/team accompanying a critically ill patient during transport) has its own distinct code structure — different from both standard critical care time-based codes and the per-day neonatal/pediatric inpatient critical care codes already discussed, reflecting the genuinely different service (transport-based critical care) being provided.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines covering observation status and split/shared visit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('dd937e95-504c-4b38-b8fb-e901348dc7c3', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 11, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding when a physician provides both a standard office visit E/M service AND separately performs advance care planning (ACP) discussion during the same visit, with each genuinely, separately documented?');
VALUES ('dd937e95-504c-4b38-b8fb-e901348dc7c3', 'fce8b080-55c4-45a9-a7d6-4b28b17b77d2', 'This reinforces the Paper 2 ACP-plus-same-day-E/M coding principle, now in the E/M-section''s own capstone context — both may be separately reportable with appropriately separate time tracking/documentation for each distinct service.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines covering observation status and split/shared visit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('0473dae0-7761-421e-b5de-573fed682d4e', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 12, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for a physician performing a hospital discharge day management service, specifically regarding what determines the appropriate code level (in code structures using a time-threshold distinction for discharge codes)?');
VALUES ('0473dae0-7761-421e-b5de-573fed682d4e', '600b4538-a964-407a-a2be-f492f16cffe5', 'Where CPT''s discharge day management codes include a time-based distinction, the actual time spent on discharge-day-specific activities (not total length of stay) determines the correct code level — reinforcing that time-based E/M coding requires tracking the SPECIFIC service''s own time, not an unrelated stay-duration metric.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines covering observation status and split/shared visit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('1359927c-d96d-49f4-973c-65c71c70460b', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 13, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding when a patient is readmitted to the same hospital for a related condition shortly after a prior discharge (e.g., within a few days), from a CODING (not payer-penalty) standpoint?');
VALUES ('1359927c-d96d-49f4-973c-65c71c70460b', '59595e42-cc69-4c45-89ad-403a6899a828', 'From a pure CODING standpoint, a readmission is its own genuine new encounter, coded per its own UHDDS/E/M principles based on the actual current reason for THIS admission — readmission-penalty program analysis (a separate payer-policy/quality-measurement concept) doesn''t change how the encounter itself is coded.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines covering observation status and split/shared visit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('cfdea009-cc00-49e6-b750-98baea65163e', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 14, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for a physician''s participation in an interdisciplinary care conference/team meeting discussing a complex patient''s care plan, without the patient being present?');
VALUES ('cfdea009-cc00-49e6-b750-98baea65163e', '8b618367-beb7-44a0-af9f-5256fe576d9a', 'This reinforces the general ''match the specific documented service/participant structure to the correct specific code family'' principle — care conference/team meeting coding depends on the specific format and participants, not automatically defaulting to a standard face-to-face E/M code regardless of the patient''s absence.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines covering observation status and split/shared visit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('6d55be03-dab7-4d03-bdf5-7166afddc5eb', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 15, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable general principle for approaching an unfamiliar E/M code-selection question involving status changes (observation-to-inpatient), split/shared billing, or discharge/readmission scenarios, synthesizing this paper with Papers 1-3?');
VALUES ('6d55be03-dab7-4d03-bdf5-7166afddc5eb', 'aa04bd8e-d5e4-40fe-acb0-d090cb4abbbb', 'This is the final E/M-section capstone synthesis across all four CPT papers — the same disciplined, documentation-specific analytical approach (status timeline, practitioner contribution, actual encounter facts) applies consistently to these more complex, real-world E/M scenarios, not a one-size-fits-all default or a system-setting shortcut.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines covering observation status and split/shared visit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('bd8296a4-7193-41d6-8307-34b1caa4b7f5', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 16, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding when a physician provides an E/M service to a patient who is a hospital inpatient of a DIFFERENT attending physician, at the request of that attending, in a payer context where consultation codes ARE recognized?');
VALUES ('bd8296a4-7193-41d6-8307-34b1caa4b7f5', 'bb393219-cbc3-4322-a17a-e2cda67001ef', 'This reinforces that consultation-code use is genuinely payer-dependent (Medicare''s non-recognition, already covered, isn''t universal across all payers) — where a payer does recognize consultation codes and the substantive criteria are met, the consulting physician appropriately reports the consultation code, billed by the CONSULTING physician, not the requester.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines covering observation status and split/shared visit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('df329ab1-4a6f-4535-b2b2-787cd6f12f49', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 17, 'Which of the following best describes the key factual difference between a ''consultation'' and a ''transfer of care,'' relevant to correct E/M code selection?');
VALUES ('df329ab1-4a6f-4535-b2b2-787cd6f12f49', '12a61866-2c20-4272-9a94-5e8f77ad03ff', 'This is a foundational, still-relevant E/M concept — consultation (opinion/advice, care remains with requester) versus transfer of care (ongoing management assumed by the second physician) reflects a genuine clinical/billing distinction affecting which E/M code family (consultation vs. standard new/established patient visit) is appropriate, in payer contexts where consultation codes remain relevant.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines covering observation status and split/shared visit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('a93d810e-3fc3-45f7-b83a-df80f6543aa3', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 18, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding when a physician who provided a consultation later assumes ongoing management of the condition they were originally asked to consult on?');
VALUES ('a93d810e-3fc3-45f7-b83a-df80f6543aa3', '698894b2-521e-4b69-94f5-b19c74d20389', 'This reinforces that E/M code selection should reflect the ACTUAL clinical relationship AT THE TIME of each specific encounter — a genuine initial consultation is coded as such, but once the relationship genuinely evolves into ongoing management, subsequent encounters reflect that new reality via standard established-patient codes, not perpetual consultation coding or retroactive re-coding of the original, accurately-characterized initial encounter.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines covering observation status and split/shared visit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('4ee1d765-3e9b-40b6-8eb6-61192bdf1a79', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 19, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable overall synthesis connecting consultation/transfer-of-care distinctions, split/shared billing, and status-change scenarios, as recurring ''relationship and timeline accuracy'' themes in E/M coding across all four papers?');
VALUES ('4ee1d765-3e9b-40b6-8eb6-61192bdf1a79', 'f6657579-9cc8-45c4-b238-4cd3649e030a', 'This is a genuine synthesis point — beyond the time/MDM leveling mechanics covered extensively elsewhere, several E/M scenarios (consultation vs. transfer of care, split/shared practitioner roles, admission/observation status timelines) require accurately characterizing WHO did WHAT and WHAT the relationship/status was AT THE RELEVANT TIME, a related but distinct coding-accuracy dimension from pure time/MDM leveling.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines covering observation status and split/shared visit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('139c7444-f98f-49f6-bfb8-fb183046313a', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 20, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding when a physician''s genuine consultation, requested by another physician, results in the consultant recommending a specific treatment plan that the REQUESTING physician then carries out themselves, with the consultant having no further involvement?');
VALUES ('139c7444-f98f-49f6-bfb8-fb183046313a', '172ca117-1ab9-4d2e-adb8-8c2f19febcc0', 'This is a clean, straightforward illustration of the consultation relationship (opinion/recommendation provided, care remains with the requester) already established — the consultant appropriately bills their own consultation code for their own distinct opinion-rendering work, regardless of who ultimately carries out the recommended treatment.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines covering observation status and split/shared visit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

-- ── Section: Anesthesia (10 questions) — Anesthesia — authored from standard CPT anesthesia section conventions covering trauma/emergency anesthesia scenarios; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank. ──

VALUES ('af46b7ee-c7ac-4652-ae72-d7140e3a8ae6', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 21, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for anesthesia provided for an emergency surgical procedure on a hemodynamically unstable trauma patient, regarding the applicable qualifying circumstances add-on code?');
VALUES ('af46b7ee-c7ac-4652-ae72-d7140e3a8ae6', '9c3494be-6f48-4181-9342-17fdf39050dd', 'This identifies the specific qualifying circumstances code for emergency conditions (delay would significantly increase threat to life/body part) — reported alongside the base anesthesia code, distinct from (though sometimes co-occurring with) physical status modifiers and age-related qualifying circumstances already discussed.', 'Anesthesia — authored from standard CPT anesthesia section conventions covering trauma/emergency anesthesia scenarios; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('7612cb12-34d0-494a-a6dd-1bd5c60d1296', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 22, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for anesthesia physical status modifier selection for a trauma patient in hemorrhagic shock requiring emergency surgery?');
VALUES ('7612cb12-34d0-494a-a6dd-1bd5c60d1296', 'e07233c3-7ab6-4e72-a66e-ef86be39c99d', 'This reinforces the physical status modifier documentation-accuracy principle already established — a genuinely unstable trauma patient''s severity should be reflected via the appropriate higher-severity P-modifier, not a default/routine low-severity selection regardless of actual documented condition.', 'Anesthesia — authored from standard CPT anesthesia section conventions covering trauma/emergency anesthesia scenarios; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('c71cd52e-450c-4426-9975-350e73b67f23', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 23, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding when anesthesia qualifying circumstances for BOTH extreme age AND emergency conditions genuinely apply to the same case (e.g., a critically injured elderly trauma patient)?');
VALUES ('c71cd52e-450c-4426-9975-350e73b67f23', 'd59c94f4-a135-446e-9f7b-76e5d33837cc', 'Multiple qualifying circumstances codes can appropriately be reported together when each is independently, genuinely documented as present — these aren''t mutually exclusive categories, and CPT''s add-on code structure accommodates reporting each qualifying circumstance actually present.', 'Anesthesia — authored from standard CPT anesthesia section conventions covering trauma/emergency anesthesia scenarios; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('f23e780a-4a58-4117-b7ef-7ef7e839e4c8', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 24, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable documentation practice to support reporting the emergency-conditions qualifying circumstances code specifically (as opposed to simply performing surgery urgently)?');
VALUES ('f23e780a-4a58-4117-b7ef-7ef7e839e4c8', '43b6e33e-7128-4a8d-9a64-6f0af8d275d4', 'This reinforces the documentation-specificity principle applied to the emergency-conditions qualifying circumstances code specifically — the code''s own defined threshold (delay significantly increasing threat to life/body part) should be clinically supported, not assumed from scheduling labels or asserted without documentation.', 'Anesthesia — authored from standard CPT anesthesia section conventions covering trauma/emergency anesthesia scenarios; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('29caaf41-d7a8-4a5d-87c6-95b895815cad', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 25, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for anesthesia provided during damage-control surgery (an abbreviated initial surgical intervention for a critically unstable trauma patient, with a planned return to the OR for definitive repair once the patient is stabilized)?');
VALUES ('29caaf41-d7a8-4a5d-87c6-95b895815cad', '9fabf1ac-6ab8-4a88-ab12-d489113b79a9', 'Each distinct operative session (initial damage-control surgery, then the later definitive-repair session) has its own anesthesia service coded based on what was actually performed at THAT session — a planned staged approach doesn''t collapse two genuinely separate anesthesia encounters into a single code.', 'Anesthesia — authored from standard CPT anesthesia section conventions covering trauma/emergency anesthesia scenarios; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('bae91a7a-0fff-40b1-8bc3-1dd21b8216bf', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 26, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable general principle for approaching an unfamiliar Anesthesia-section code-selection question specifically involving trauma/emergency scenarios, synthesizing this paper with Papers 1-3?');
VALUES ('bae91a7a-0fff-40b1-8bc3-1dd21b8216bf', '1c11ee6a-66aa-4b13-93b7-9a60bd63d043', 'This is a capstone synthesis for trauma/emergency anesthesia coding — the same core framework (base code, physical status modifier, qualifying circumstances) already established throughout this content applies, with trauma/emergency scenarios often involving multiple genuinely applicable qualifying circumstances simultaneously, not a wholly separate coding system.', 'Anesthesia — authored from standard CPT anesthesia section conventions covering trauma/emergency anesthesia scenarios; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('9d8a2fdf-429f-4e34-9563-f9ef0171abe0', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 27, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for anesthesia provided for a cesarean delivery in a case of maternal hemorrhagic emergency (e.g., placental abruption with fetal distress)?');
VALUES ('9d8a2fdf-429f-4e34-9563-f9ef0171abe0', 'a94cfa6b-745f-439b-a485-229b9646071c', 'This connects two previously-separate concepts (obstetric anesthesia coding from Paper 2, and emergency-conditions qualifying circumstances from this paper) — both may appropriately apply together when a genuine obstetric emergency meets the specific documented threshold for the qualifying circumstances code.', 'Anesthesia — authored from standard CPT anesthesia section conventions covering trauma/emergency anesthesia scenarios; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('8bb56029-74a8-4b80-a949-16c3d8f4caa6', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 28, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable documentation practice when anesthesia is provided for a mass casualty or multi-trauma scenario involving rapid sequential procedures on the same patient?');
VALUES ('8bb56029-74a8-4b80-a949-16c3d8f4caa6', 'b808eba9-12d3-48b7-b3c4-7af9c7a81fa3', 'Even amid genuinely chaotic emergency circumstances, the same underlying documentation-specificity principles apply — accurate coding still depends on correctly delineating and documenting each distinct procedure/anesthesia episode, not abandoning documentation rigor due to the scenario''s urgency.', 'Anesthesia — authored from standard CPT anesthesia section conventions covering trauma/emergency anesthesia scenarios; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('d22523ab-122c-44e7-aef2-6d44d28a15ed', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 29, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for anesthesia provided for interventional radiology procedures performed emergently (e.g., emergency embolization for uncontrolled hemorrhage)?');
VALUES ('d22523ab-122c-44e7-aef2-6d44d28a15ed', 'ecc9476b-aeef-488e-a5d5-629466ea006c', 'This connects interventional radiology anesthesia coding (implicit in Paper 3''s IR content) with the emergency-qualifying-circumstances concept — both apply based on their own respective, independent documentation criteria (procedure/region for the base code, genuine emergency threshold for the qualifying circumstance), regardless of whether the underlying procedure is open surgical or interventional/minimally invasive.', 'Anesthesia — authored from standard CPT anesthesia section conventions covering trauma/emergency anesthesia scenarios; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('8f8cf9b6-d2fd-48ab-af76-da5a764eb95e', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 30, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable compliance consideration for reporting qualifying circumstances add-on codes (emergency conditions, extreme age, hypothermia, controlled hypotension) across a facility''s overall anesthesia billing pattern?');
VALUES ('8f8cf9b6-d2fd-48ab-af76-da5a764eb95e', '7a33f4c2-e483-4c04-aa5d-8e620be359b8', 'This reinforces the recurring documentation-support/compliance-auditing theme (already established for POA, ventilator hours, device specificity, and other complexity-affecting coding elements throughout this content) applied specifically to anesthesia qualifying circumstances codes — genuine documentation support, not routine default application, should govern their use.', 'Anesthesia — authored from standard CPT anesthesia section conventions covering trauma/emergency anesthesia scenarios; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

-- ── Section: Surgery (30 questions) — Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering neurosurgery/spine and hernia repair; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank. ──

VALUES ('3c4f58e5-dbbd-4715-bfc6-46bf6c3035b0', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 31, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for a craniotomy performed for tumor resection, as distinguished from a craniotomy performed for evacuation of a traumatic hematoma (already discussed generally in Paper 2)?');
VALUES ('3c4f58e5-dbbd-4715-bfc6-46bf6c3035b0', '22caf390-2058-4826-ad2c-4ad3e06244b2', 'This reinforces the recurring ''code the specific indication/procedure performed'' principle applied to craniotomy — different indications (tumor vs. hematoma vs. other) generally map to different specific codes reflecting the actual procedure performed through the craniotomy, not a single generic code.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering neurosurgery/spine and hernia repair; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('f1505fe5-bf54-4843-baa3-7f9096a7ecd4', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 32, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for stereotactic biopsy of a brain lesion, as distinguished from an open craniotomy biopsy?');
VALUES ('f1505fe5-bf54-4843-baa3-7f9096a7ecd4', '45371876-3de0-42ea-8ff0-c694004856ff', 'This reinforces the approach-specificity coding theme (already established for cholecystectomy, rotator cuff repair, carpal tunnel release, and others) applied to brain lesion biopsy — stereotactic and open approaches are genuinely different techniques with distinct codes.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering neurosurgery/spine and hernia repair; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('a8ce48e0-0139-4913-a3ed-6948cf937d5a', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 33, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for a laminectomy performed for spinal stenosis, as distinguished from a laminectomy performed as part of a spinal fusion procedure at the same session?');
VALUES ('a8ce48e0-0139-4913-a3ed-6948cf937d5a', '7a141ecc-a4d5-43ac-a5ed-5b68e3e0b88e', 'This reapplies the general bundling-analysis principle (checking specific code descriptors/NCCI edits rather than assuming a blanket rule) to laminectomy-plus-fusion scenarios — a standalone decompression laminectomy (no fusion) is coded on its own, while combined with fusion requires checking the specific applicable bundling rules for that combination.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering neurosurgery/spine and hernia repair; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('2bc3b165-1e6b-4203-8111-796e12655f2a', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 34, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for anterior versus posterior spinal fusion approach, as a code-selection factor?');
VALUES ('2bc3b165-1e6b-4203-8111-796e12655f2a', 'dd9f7558-518c-4bd2-9cf9-bd950600008d', 'This reinforces the approach-specificity theme applied to spinal fusion specifically — surgical approach (anterior/posterior/lateral) is a genuine, additional code-selection factor alongside the already-discussed level-count factor, not an either/or replacement for it.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering neurosurgery/spine and hernia repair; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('572b0a8c-301b-4945-8972-631592828ded', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 35, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for insertion of spinal instrumentation (e.g., pedicle screws and rods) as part of a spinal fusion procedure?');
VALUES ('572b0a8c-301b-4945-8972-631592828ded', '602220a2-db62-49a7-8711-94a94f1fe677', 'This reapplies the general base-procedure-plus-device-component coding pattern to spinal instrumentation — a separately identifiable, commonly add-on-coded component of the overall fusion procedure, not automatically bundled into the base fusion code.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering neurosurgery/spine and hernia repair; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('05db692c-978d-4ca3-87e4-3adcc312b89a', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 36, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for an inguinal hernia repair, specifically regarding the distinction between initial and recurrent hernia repair?');
VALUES ('05db692c-978d-4ca3-87e4-3adcc312b89a', '500fafd0-2d76-49e0-b954-f8a4b327c4ce', 'This reinforces the recurring ''documented complexity/history affects code selection'' theme applied to hernia repair — initial versus recurrent status is a genuine, commonly-coded distinction, not an interchangeable single code regardless of surgical history.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering neurosurgery/spine and hernia repair; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('f2e84f5e-0ef8-4e4b-b024-3ec821dca1c6', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 37, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for hernia repair performed with mesh reinforcement, as distinguished from repair without mesh?');
