VALUES ('3247fc1a-7edb-440e-86e6-5d03c2f071cd', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 62, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for a PET/CT study performed for oncologic staging, as distinguished from a PET/CT performed for cardiac viability assessment?');
VALUES ('3247fc1a-7edb-440e-86e6-5d03c2f071cd', 'd9b5da9a-1932-476f-bb68-51636f58d4cb', 'This reinforces that imaging PURPOSE/indication and body region (not just the general technology used) are defining code-selection factors — a theme already established for CTA/MRA versus routine CT/MRI, now applied to PET/CT''s own indication-specific code structure.', 'Radiology — authored from standard CPT Radiology section conventions covering MRI/PET contrast conventions; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('a749cd39-5534-401a-baa7-614e2cd863fd', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 63, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for the radiopharmaceutical (e.g., FDG) used in a PET scan, as distinguished from the PET imaging procedure code itself?');
VALUES ('a749cd39-5534-401a-baa7-614e2cd863fd', '4d1d516f-ee0c-44ea-8a1f-5553c92f7d21', 'This reapplies the Paper 2 nuclear medicine product-plus-service separation principle specifically to PET imaging — the tracer/radiopharmaceutical and the imaging procedure are separately identifiable components, consistent with the broader pattern already established.', 'Radiology — authored from standard CPT Radiology section conventions covering MRI/PET contrast conventions; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('c517959a-a208-4d8f-b5a6-a5fe8ed868d3', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 64, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for functional MRI (fMRI), typically used for pre-surgical brain mapping, as distinguished from a routine structural brain MRI?');
VALUES ('c517959a-a208-4d8f-b5a6-a5fe8ed868d3', '2a730b64-698e-4aaf-bfa9-71abd2893645', 'This reinforces the imaging-PURPOSE/protocol-specificity theme with another concrete example — fMRI (functional) and routine structural MRI capture fundamentally different information despite using the same underlying MRI technology, and each has its own distinct code.', 'Radiology — authored from standard CPT Radiology section conventions covering MRI/PET contrast conventions; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('a2435546-6f25-411b-9764-4763b9fc998b', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 65, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for MRI-guided breast biopsy, as distinguished from ultrasound-guided or stereotactic (mammography-guided) breast biopsy already discussed in Paper 2?');
VALUES ('a2435546-6f25-411b-9764-4763b9fc998b', 'b5f5ee24-43dd-4c44-b9c5-63bfeeb3395d', 'This extends the Paper 2 breast-biopsy-guidance-modality principle with a third distinct guidance option (MRI, alongside ultrasound and stereotactic) — reinforcing that the SPECIFIC guidance modality actually used and documented determines correct code selection.', 'Radiology — authored from standard CPT Radiology section conventions covering MRI/PET contrast conventions; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('8c7461e1-da63-488f-9b6e-0bfb578e5f9e', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 66, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable documentation practice to support correct coding when distinguishing an MRI/PET study''s specific contrast protocol, functional versus structural purpose, or guidance modality, synthesizing themes from across this content?');
VALUES ('8c7461e1-da63-488f-9b6e-0bfb578e5f9e', '161d1b9e-43b7-47de-8e78-fdb6acf60839', 'This is a capstone synthesis of the documentation-specificity theme applied to Radiology-section coding broadly (across contrast protocols, functional vs. structural purpose, and guidance modality) — accurate coding consistently depends on documented protocol/purpose specifics beyond just modality and body part.', 'Radiology — authored from standard CPT Radiology section conventions covering MRI/PET contrast conventions; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('d065ce78-bdbe-448d-b970-0c6e2d3768bd', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 67, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for a PET scan performed to evaluate suspected dementia/cognitive decline, as distinguished from an oncologic PET scan?');
VALUES ('d065ce78-bdbe-448d-b970-0c6e2d3768bd', 'a499672f-1cbd-4b48-9097-ea80144da473', 'This reinforces the indication/radiopharmaceutical-specificity theme for PET imaging with a genuinely distinct clinical example (neurologic/dementia evaluation, potentially using an entirely different specialized radiopharmaceutical) from the oncologic PET/CT scenario already discussed.', 'Radiology — authored from standard CPT Radiology section conventions covering MRI/PET contrast conventions; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('4c6c021c-5f59-45bf-b029-6cdfba55a6de', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 68, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for an MRI arthrogram (MRI performed after injection of contrast directly into a joint), as distinguished from a routine, non-arthrographic joint MRI?');
VALUES ('4c6c021c-5f59-45bf-b029-6cdfba55a6de', 'e8e2c5da-0a7b-4350-8494-b1c402b8c1e6', 'This reapplies the combined-versus-component code-verification discipline to MRI arthrography specifically — a procedure combining an injection component with a specific imaging protocol, requiring the same descriptor-matching analysis already established as a transferable skill throughout this content.', 'Radiology — authored from standard CPT Radiology section conventions covering MRI/PET contrast conventions; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('876fae00-ef6e-4a5a-ab75-faef9877aad0', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 69, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable compliance consideration for a facility performing high volumes of advanced imaging (MRI, CT, PET) with contrast, given the FDA/clinical safety considerations around contrast agents (e.g., gadolinium, iodinated contrast)?');
VALUES ('876fae00-ef6e-4a5a-ab75-faef9877aad0', '5674e5a9-0993-4041-9ac3-20e23343f93b', 'This connects clinical contrast-safety practice (screening for renal function/allergy history) to the broader documentation-supports-coding theme — while primarily a patient-safety matter, this documentation also supports medical-necessity justification for the specific contrast-enhanced study being billed.', 'Radiology — authored from standard CPT Radiology section conventions covering MRI/PET contrast conventions; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('b250d750-e17d-4197-bf19-30c61daef94d', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 70, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable general principle for approaching an unfamiliar Radiology-section code-selection question specifically involving MRI/PET protocol variations, synthesizing this paper with Papers 1-3?');
VALUES ('b250d750-e17d-4197-bf19-30c61daef94d', '2cf701cd-442b-4a1b-9657-3203ada1d655', 'This is the final Radiology-section capstone synthesis across all four papers — the same disciplined, protocol-specific descriptor-matching approach (modality, contrast, purpose, guidance, combined-structure) applies consistently, reinforcing the transferable analytical skill established throughout this content''s Radiology-section coverage.', 'Radiology — authored from standard CPT Radiology section conventions covering MRI/PET contrast conventions; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

-- ── Section: Pathology & Laboratory (10 questions) — Pathology & Laboratory — authored from standard CPT Pathology & Laboratory section conventions covering chemistry/endocrine panels; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank. ──

VALUES ('e374e20e-6cd2-49b9-a884-330c70636fc3', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 71, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for a thyroid function panel (e.g., TSH, free T4) ordered to evaluate suspected hypothyroidism, as distinguished from ordering TSH alone?');
VALUES ('e374e20e-6cd2-49b9-a884-330c70636fc3', '0c90f361-7847-40b3-9f65-0d4aaf2c4146', 'This reapplies the general panel-completeness principle (already established for BMP/CMP and other defined panels) to thyroid testing — the actual tests performed (individual TSH vs. a complete defined panel) determine whether the panel code or individual component code(s) apply.', 'Pathology & Laboratory — authored from standard CPT Pathology & Laboratory section conventions covering chemistry/endocrine panels; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('af59de4c-77f3-4c90-8b96-ef0bd44b61d2', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 72, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for a hemoglobin A1c (HbA1c) test, used to assess long-term blood glucose control in diabetic patients, as distinguished from a fasting blood glucose test?');
VALUES ('af59de4c-77f3-4c90-8b96-ef0bd44b61d2', 'adb83226-ccd4-43e9-a79c-5eff2879a7da', 'HbA1c (long-term average glycemic control) and fasting glucose (point-in-time level) measure genuinely different things despite both being diabetes-monitoring-relevant — each with its own distinct code, reinforcing that the SPECIFIC analyte/parameter measured (not just the general clinical purpose) determines correct code selection.', 'Pathology & Laboratory — authored from standard CPT Pathology & Laboratory section conventions covering chemistry/endocrine panels; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('6474c0e3-f0ad-4594-9aca-401803346288', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 73, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for a lipid panel (typically including total cholesterol, HDL, LDL, and triglycerides), regarding the panel-completeness principle already established?');
VALUES ('6474c0e3-f0ad-4594-9aca-401803346288', '4ae48103-3a27-4716-82f1-fbaa3c0fd067', 'This reapplies the panel-completeness principle yet again to lipid panels specifically — reinforcing this as a genuinely general laboratory-panel-coding rule (already demonstrated for BMP/CMP and thyroid panels), not a rule specific to any single panel type.', 'Pathology & Laboratory — authored from standard CPT Pathology & Laboratory section conventions covering chemistry/endocrine panels; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('11d8db09-dc5d-4e9a-bed1-c6b06978de01', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 74, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for a parathyroid hormone (PTH) test, ordered to evaluate calcium metabolism disorders, as distinguished from a total/ionized calcium test?');
VALUES ('11d8db09-dc5d-4e9a-bed1-c6b06978de01', '39b77881-a262-47dc-a328-53a8be087586', 'This reinforces the distinct-analyte-distinct-code principle for endocrine/metabolic testing — PTH and calcium are separately coded despite frequent joint clinical interpretation, and (per the question''s own framing) total versus ionized calcium also represent distinct, separately coded measurement methods.', 'Pathology & Laboratory — authored from standard CPT Pathology & Laboratory section conventions covering chemistry/endocrine panels; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('ad78de65-327a-4fe4-b321-f3ad7de80fd2', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 75, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for cortisol testing performed at multiple specific times of day (e.g., AM and PM cortisol) to evaluate suspected adrenal dysfunction?');
VALUES ('ad78de65-327a-4fe4-b321-f3ad7de80fd2', '8ca9fe91-c221-4493-9233-70cf5b92fdce', 'This reinforces the general ''code reflects the actual number of distinct tests/specimens performed'' principle applied to timed hormone testing — each clinically-specified separate draw/measurement is its own separately codeable test, not artificially combined into a single code regardless of protocol.', 'Pathology & Laboratory — authored from standard CPT Pathology & Laboratory section conventions covering chemistry/endocrine panels; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('8deab9d9-c2b8-4b4d-b2f8-eab6dfccd2d4', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 76, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for a glucose tolerance test (GTT), involving a baseline glucose measurement followed by additional measurements at specified time intervals after a glucose challenge?');
VALUES ('8deab9d9-c2b8-4b4d-b2f8-eab6dfccd2d4', '585fa21a-1d8f-4ee4-93d8-01f42d2fdbec', 'GTT has its own defined, protocol-specific code structure (reflecting the multi-timepoint glucose-challenge protocol as a distinct test), rather than being built from repeated individual glucose codes — a specific laboratory test STRUCTURE distinct from simply repeating a component test multiple times.', 'Pathology & Laboratory — authored from standard CPT Pathology & Laboratory section conventions covering chemistry/endocrine panels; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('d13d4c74-b0b1-49e0-b349-3a8ffa0f10fd', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 77, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable documentation practice to support correct coding for endocrine/metabolic panel or timed-testing scenarios (thyroid panels, GTT, timed cortisol), synthesizing themes established throughout this content''s Pathology & Laboratory coverage?');
VALUES ('d13d4c74-b0b1-49e0-b349-3a8ffa0f10fd', 'fca179c0-bce2-43bc-a240-4291e60719a7', 'This is a capstone synthesis applying the recurring Pathology & Laboratory documentation-specificity theme (already established for panels, reflex testing, and toxicology) to endocrine/metabolic testing specifically — the same underlying principle, not a distinct standard.', 'Pathology & Laboratory — authored from standard CPT Pathology & Laboratory section conventions covering chemistry/endocrine panels; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('0ceaa1ab-c5ab-415d-840d-d82c049e1b70', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 78, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for a tumor marker test (e.g., PSA for prostate cancer monitoring, CA-125 for ovarian cancer monitoring), as distinguished from a general chemistry panel component?');
VALUES ('0ceaa1ab-c5ab-415d-840d-d82c049e1b70', '324fe026-8806-428d-8f37-99b08076169f', 'Tumor markers are specialized, marker-specific codes (PSA, CA-125, and others each having their own distinct code) — structurally separate from general chemistry panels, reinforcing that the SPECIFIC analyte (not a broad category like ''tumor marker'' or ''chemistry test'') determines correct code selection.', 'Pathology & Laboratory — authored from standard CPT Pathology & Laboratory section conventions covering chemistry/endocrine panels; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('c56db6a4-9fd5-4d47-abc9-aeb3445dbc34', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 79, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding when a screening PSA test (routine, asymptomatic screening) is later followed by a diagnostic PSA test (monitoring a patient with known prostate cancer) at a different encounter?');
VALUES ('c56db6a4-9fd5-4d47-abc9-aeb3445dbc34', 'b2edf9ef-2c17-40f5-a279-4f435995819b', 'This reapplies the screening-versus-diagnostic coding/coverage distinction (already established for mammography and colonoscopy) to PSA testing specifically — the same underlying laboratory test can have different coding/coverage treatment depending on the actual clinical purpose of the specific encounter.', 'Pathology & Laboratory — authored from standard CPT Pathology & Laboratory section conventions covering chemistry/endocrine panels; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('1b733363-9d98-4f57-83ef-2e3e94d163d3', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 80, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable general principle for approaching an unfamiliar Pathology & Laboratory code-selection question specifically involving endocrine/metabolic or tumor marker testing, synthesizing this paper with Papers 1-3?');
VALUES ('1b733363-9d98-4f57-83ef-2e3e94d163d3', '6c892cc2-13f7-46c5-9322-e3b31521b93a', 'This is the final Pathology & Laboratory capstone synthesis across all four papers — the same disciplined analytical approach (specific analyte verification, panel-completeness, distinct-analyte recognition, protocol-structure awareness, screening-vs-diagnostic purpose) applies consistently across every laboratory subsection covered in this content.', 'Pathology & Laboratory — authored from standard CPT Pathology & Laboratory section conventions covering chemistry/endocrine panels; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

-- ── Section: Medicine (10 questions) — Medicine — authored from standard CPT Medicine section conventions covering physical/occupational therapy timed codes; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank. ──

VALUES ('0ce47c15-221c-414e-b9eb-a67806f80d19', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 81, 'Which of the following best describes the ''8-minute rule'' concept referenced generally in Paper 2''s discussion of timed physical therapy codes?');
VALUES ('0ce47c15-221c-414e-b9eb-a67806f80d19', 'ea5bcd0c-02c1-4cf0-8234-a99149ea5bd5', 'The 8-minute rule is specifically a unit-calculation convention for timed CPT codes — translating total documented minutes into billable units via defined minute thresholds, not a rule about session length itself or code selection unrelated to time.', 'Medicine — authored from standard CPT Medicine section conventions covering physical/occupational therapy timed codes; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('c140908e-9382-40cd-91f3-7716e46a6abc', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 82, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding when a physical therapy session includes both a timed code (e.g., therapeutic exercise, billed per 15-minute unit) AND an untimed, service-based code (e.g., an unattended electrical stimulation modality) during the same visit?');
VALUES ('c140908e-9382-40cd-91f3-7716e46a6abc', 'f134f9c7-aac7-4dbd-9cc8-dc81ff1adae5', 'This reapplies the timed-versus-untimed billing-structure distinction (already established generally) to a concrete same-visit scenario — both types of codes may be separately reported per their own respective structures (units-by-minutes vs. once-per-service) when both services were genuinely, distinctly performed.', 'Medicine — authored from standard CPT Medicine section conventions covering physical/occupational therapy timed codes; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('c34c1dfb-a340-4b23-bee7-2a01b50a1405', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 83, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for therapeutic exercise versus neuromuscular reeducation, two distinct timed physical therapy codes?');
VALUES ('c34c1dfb-a340-4b23-bee7-2a01b50a1405', '549ed91a-54e3-4af2-8063-18255369a46c', 'This reinforces the recurring ''broad category contains multiple genuinely distinct, specifically-coded techniques'' theme (already seen for audiology, hernia repair, and other areas) applied to physical therapy — technique performed (not body part alone) determines correct code selection between these two timed code options.', 'Medicine — authored from standard CPT Medicine section conventions covering physical/occupational therapy timed codes; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('d1839059-77ef-4f7d-92af-e263b9514f5a', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 84, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for manual therapy techniques (e.g., joint mobilization, soft tissue mobilization) performed by a physical therapist, as distinguished from therapeutic exercise?');
VALUES ('d1839059-77ef-4f7d-92af-e263b9514f5a', 'fc72341e-e997-461e-9c60-14484789ada4', 'This further reinforces the distinct-technique-distinct-code principle within physical therapy coding — manual therapy (hands-on practitioner technique) is genuinely different from both therapeutic exercise (active patient exercise) and neuromuscular reeducation (movement/balance retraining), each with its own specific code.', 'Medicine — authored from standard CPT Medicine section conventions covering physical/occupational therapy timed codes; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('de74b73f-9479-4055-8e61-920e8ea0b3f4', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 85, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for a physical therapy evaluation, as distinguished from ongoing timed treatment codes (therapeutic exercise, manual therapy, etc.)?');
VALUES ('de74b73f-9479-4055-8e61-920e8ea0b3f4', 'be1ce846-9173-4da1-a7a8-901965362f65', 'This reinforces the recurring evaluation-versus-ongoing-treatment coding distinction (already seen for psychiatric diagnostic evaluation vs. psychotherapy in Paper 1) applied to physical therapy — evaluation and ongoing timed treatment are structurally distinct code categories, with evaluation complexity itself being a documented, code-selection-relevant factor where the code structure provides tiers.', 'Medicine — authored from standard CPT Medicine section conventions covering physical/occupational therapy timed codes; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('f0aa0cdc-d2b8-4604-9934-dedd74fbec07', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 86, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for occupational therapy (OT) codes addressing activities of daily living (ADL) training, as distinguished from physical therapy''s therapeutic exercise or manual therapy codes?');
VALUES ('f0aa0cdc-d2b8-4604-9934-dedd74fbec07', '01a61f8f-7e80-4f20-88c7-823f48bbfcb1', 'OT''s code set overlaps substantially with PT''s timed/untimed code family (both disciplines can bill many of the same codes, like therapeutic exercise) while also including OT-specific codes (ADL/self-care training) — reflecting a nuanced, partially-shared-but-not-identical code relationship between these related but distinct disciplines, governed also by state scope-of-practice rules.', 'Medicine — authored from standard CPT Medicine section conventions covering physical/occupational therapy timed codes; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('e3e32d7e-ebe4-4367-9ed2-75ba8595426b', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 87, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable documentation practice to support the correct number of units billed for a timed physical/occupational therapy code, per the 8-minute rule concept?');
VALUES ('e3e32d7e-ebe4-4367-9ed2-75ba8595426b', 'fb79443f-df36-44f6-b22f-6ead0c507496', 'This reinforces the recurring time-documentation principle (already established for many other time-based code families throughout this content) applied specifically to the 8-minute-rule unit-calculation context — genuine, specific minute documentation (not a flat assumption or intensity-based subjective judgment) supports accurate unit billing.', 'Medicine — authored from standard CPT Medicine section conventions covering physical/occupational therapy timed codes; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('6961a67c-51ef-4985-a671-f72645786a9f', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 88, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable compliance consideration for a therapy practice billing multiple timed-code units per visit across many patients, given the 8-minute rule''s direct connection between documented minutes and billed units?');
VALUES ('6961a67c-51ef-4985-a671-f72645786a9f', 'b770e5d3-2625-4ad8-88be-92bcd9ca5542', 'This reapplies the targeted-focused-audit rationale (already established for other documentation-dependent, unit/duration-based coding areas) to timed therapy billing specifically — a direct, mathematical time-to-units relationship makes this a reasonable, efficient area for periodic compliance verification.', 'Medicine — authored from standard CPT Medicine section conventions covering physical/occupational therapy timed codes; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('a7f97cd8-eae4-4a6d-9ea3-ac5fa074996a', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 89, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding when a patient receives group physical therapy (multiple patients performing therapeutic exercises simultaneously under one therapist''s supervision), as distinguished from one-on-one, individual therapy?');
VALUES ('a7f97cd8-eae4-4a6d-9ea3-ac5fa074996a', '95575ea4-a40e-46a6-b8a4-694310eae664', 'This reinforces the general service-STRUCTURE-affects-code-selection theme (already seen for split/shared visits, group medical visits, and other format-dependent scenarios) applied to physical therapy — group therapy''s distinct supervision structure (versus one-on-one contact) warrants its own specific code, not identical treatment to individual timed codes.', 'Medicine — authored from standard CPT Medicine section conventions covering physical/occupational therapy timed codes; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('31f60afe-150a-488e-bcc3-d4b5c380e4ec', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 90, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable general principle for approaching an unfamiliar Medicine-section code-selection question specifically involving physical/occupational therapy services, synthesizing this paper with Papers 1-3?');
VALUES ('31f60afe-150a-488e-bcc3-d4b5c380e4ec', '460374fd-28d6-4e2a-982a-4eefe5877857', 'This is the final Medicine-section capstone synthesis for physical/occupational therapy coding, connecting to the broader Medicine-section analytical discipline already established across all four papers — the same underlying approach (specific service structure, accurate time documentation, correct code matching) applies consistently.', 'Medicine — authored from standard CPT Medicine section conventions covering physical/occupational therapy timed codes; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

-- ── Section: Modifiers & NCCI (10 questions) — Modifiers & NCCI — authored from standard CPT modifier definitions covering global-period-specific scenarios and CMS NCCI edit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank. ──

VALUES ('7910b0f9-b2a1-48d2-b089-21a5cda10c78', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 91, 'Which of the following best describes the general concept of the ''global period'' assigned to a surgical CPT code (e.g., 0-day, 10-day, or 90-day global periods)?');
VALUES ('7910b0f9-b2a1-48d2-b089-21a5cda10c78', '8d6c9a71-105c-4aff-952f-a6168d5aca1b', 'The global period is the defined postoperative window (varying by procedure, 0/10/90 days being common categories) during which routine follow-up care is bundled — and it''s precisely BECAUSE of the global period concept that modifiers 24/58/78/79 exist, to correctly flag services that fall within this window but should be separately payable.', 'Modifiers & NCCI — authored from standard CPT modifier definitions covering global-period-specific scenarios and CMS NCCI edit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('27135017-574a-471b-a110-526de02b8775', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 92, 'Which of the following best describes why modifier 24 (unrelated E/M service during a postoperative period) specifically requires the E/M service to be UNRELATED to the original procedure?');
VALUES ('27135017-574a-471b-a110-526de02b8775', '50e32066-734b-4bef-a472-a369fbbd1abf', 'This reinforces WHY modifier 24 specifically requires unrelatedness — the global period''s bundling logic already covers related follow-up, so modifier 24''s entire purpose is flagging the genuine EXCEPTION (an unrelated condition), not providing a general bypass for any postoperative E/M service regardless of relatedness.', 'Modifiers & NCCI — authored from standard CPT modifier definitions covering global-period-specific scenarios and CMS NCCI edit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('26f2eaf6-317a-4ca9-89a9-c61de1d8eead', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 93, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding when a patient develops a complication genuinely UNRELATED to their recent surgery (e.g., a new pneumonia diagnosis) during the postoperative global period, requiring an E/M visit?');
VALUES ('26f2eaf6-317a-4ca9-89a9-c61de1d8eead', '663d3720-3225-45ba-afe9-b3b85b8bea34', 'This is a clean, direct application of modifier 24''s defined purpose — a genuinely unrelated new condition arising during the global period is exactly the scenario this modifier exists to flag for separate payment, distinct from modifier 58 (which addresses a RELATED staged procedure, not an unrelated E/M visit).', 'Modifiers & NCCI — authored from standard CPT modifier definitions covering global-period-specific scenarios and CMS NCCI edit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('0187ce06-7821-4f7c-a3a5-69831f468b6d', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 94, 'Which of the following best describes the compliance risk associated with inappropriately appending modifier 24 to an E/M service that is, in fact, RELATED to the recent surgery (i.e., routine postoperative follow-up)?');
VALUES ('0187ce06-7821-4f7c-a3a5-69831f468b6d', 'f8b5673f-754d-4429-a91a-3ed1d65bf715', 'This reinforces the general modifier-misuse compliance-risk pattern (already established for NCCI-bypass modifiers) applied specifically to modifier 24 — inappropriately flagging a genuinely related service as ''unrelated'' to bypass global-period bundling is the same underlying compliance risk (seeking payment for something that should already be included), just via a different modifier.', 'Modifiers & NCCI — authored from standard CPT modifier definitions covering global-period-specific scenarios and CMS NCCI edit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('97b69d9d-8fef-4121-8850-5398feeacec2', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 95, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable documentation practice to support appropriate use of modifier 24 specifically?');
VALUES ('97b69d9d-8fef-4121-8850-5398feeacec2', '34ec8025-edf9-4ed9-807a-764ee04ef0c3', 'This reinforces the documentation-support principle applied specifically to modifier 24 — genuine clinical unrelatedness (not just occurring during the global period, and not the same documentation focus as modifier 58''s staged-relatedness concept) is what needs to be clearly established.', 'Modifiers & NCCI — authored from standard CPT modifier definitions covering global-period-specific scenarios and CMS NCCI edit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('421f1899-122e-4057-bf27-b6f2f04cbf61', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 96, 'Which of the following best describes the distinction between modifiers 78 and 79, both addressing procedures during a postoperative global period, as a comprehensive review connecting Paper 1''s introduction of these modifiers?');
VALUES ('421f1899-122e-4057-bf27-b6f2f04cbf61', '01775564-e2c6-43d3-9f0d-31aed30d418e', 'This is a comprehensive review of the 78-versus-79 distinction already introduced in Paper 1 — related-but-unplanned (78) versus unrelated (79) is the key differentiator, with facility location being irrelevant to either modifier''s proper use.', 'Modifiers & NCCI — authored from standard CPT modifier definitions covering global-period-specific scenarios and CMS NCCI edit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('3234bb7f-b772-4b93-bdb3-17c77ccf0911', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 97, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding when a planned, staged second procedure (modifier 58) is performed during the global period, and this second procedure ITSELF has its own new global period?');
VALUES ('3234bb7f-b772-4b93-bdb3-17c77ccf0911', '755b5574-2485-486a-8a00-5fed73a49d61', 'This is a genuinely nuanced but important global-period concept — a new procedure (whether via modifier 58''s staged/planned pathway or modifier 78''s unplanned-return pathway) generally establishes its OWN new global period, meaning the ''clock'' for evaluating subsequent related/unrelated services resets relative to the MOST RECENT procedure, not perpetually tied to the original one.', 'Modifiers & NCCI — authored from standard CPT modifier definitions covering global-period-specific scenarios and CMS NCCI edit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('e7b3ba6a-12bb-4e57-bc6f-ba5337cdbd40', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 98, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable documentation practice to support tracking global period status across a patient with multiple staged/related procedures over time (e.g., a multi-stage breast reconstruction, already discussed generally in Paper 2)?');
VALUES ('e7b3ba6a-12bb-4e57-bc6f-ba5337cdbd40', 'b2a6959a-d286-4f03-884b-8397a4839181', 'This connects the global-period-restart concept to the Paper 2 staged-breast-reconstruction example — clear procedure-relationship documentation over time supports both correct modifier selection at each step and accurate understanding of which global period is currently ''active'' for evaluating subsequent services.', 'Modifiers & NCCI — authored from standard CPT modifier definitions covering global-period-specific scenarios and CMS NCCI edit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('648cefb7-bb2d-43d4-a0a2-70f091afdfa7', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 99, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable compliance/audit consideration for a surgical practice''s use of global-period-related modifiers (24, 58, 78, 79) in aggregate across its patient population?');
VALUES ('648cefb7-bb2d-43d4-a0a2-70f091afdfa7', '39a26fc2-0603-4634-a003-912bfc29bc14', 'This reapplies the general modifier-outcome-pattern-monitoring compliance principle (already established for query outcomes and NCCI-bypass modifiers) to the global-period modifier family specifically — genuine documentation-supported use, not reflexive/routine application, is what compliance monitoring should verify.', 'Modifiers & NCCI — authored from standard CPT modifier definitions covering global-period-specific scenarios and CMS NCCI edit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('8a91ae38-b642-4162-908b-63a584857cb8', '161a5441-b93a-4090-955b-3ea097d0b2fe', 100, 'Which of the following best describes the single most important overarching lesson connecting global-period modifiers (24, 58, 78, 79), NCCI-bypass modifiers (59/X{EPSU}), and physician-role modifiers (62, 66, 80/81/82), as a final synthesis across this entire four-paper CPT series'' Modifiers & NCCI content?');
VALUES ('8a91ae38-b642-4162-908b-63a584857cb8', '2bcf7bca-7b50-4eaf-88c1-99eeb1d114aa', 'This is the true final synthesis for the entire four-paper CPT series'' modifier content — despite addressing genuinely different circumstances (relatedness/timing, clinical distinctness, physician role), every modifier category shares the same underlying principle (accurately communicate genuine documented facts) and the same underlying compliance risk (reflexive/reimbursement-driven misuse rather than genuine documentation support) — the single most transferable lesson from this entire content area.', 'Modifiers & NCCI — authored from standard CPT modifier definitions covering global-period-specific scenarios and CMS NCCI edit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');
